# patch-rationale.md (Patcher's emitted rationale)
## Upstream reference

The fix is recorded in `upstream-ref.json.release_url` and
`upstream_fix_commits[0]`: https://github.com/openbsd/src commit
`62ceddea5b1d64a1a362bbb7071d9e15adcde6b1` — `x509_constraints_parse_mailbox`
bounds the `working[]` copy loop with `>=` instead of `>` so a 256-byte
domain part can no longer fill the buffer without leaving room for the
`strdup()` terminator.

## Why this version

`upstream-ref.json.target_version` equals `current_version` (3.4.0) because
no qualifying LibreSSL release in the pinned channel carries the fix at
this rev; the resolver authorized `patch_strategy = source-backport`.

## Why minimal

Two paths are touched, both under
`pkgs/development/libraries/libressl/`: a new `CVE-2021-41581.patch` file
transcribing the upstream `crypto/x509/x509_constraints.c` hunk verbatim,
and a one-line addition of `./CVE-2021-41581.patch` to the `libressl_3_4`
generic call's `patches = [ ];` list. No version, hash, fetcher, or
unrelated attribute is modified.

The transcribed hunk preserves the full compound guard at the end of
`x509_constraints_parse_mailbox`:
`if (candidate_local == NULL || candidate_domain == NULL) goto bad;`
Both conjuncts must remain — dropping either would let the function accept
a mailbox for which the corresponding half (local part or domain part)
was never actually parsed, reintroducing a use of an uninitialized/NULL
`name->local` or `name->name`.

## Alternatives rejected

- Version bump to 3.4.1: rejected — 3.4.1 is not the resolver's target and
  would pull in unrelated release changes, widening the diff beyond the
  single CVE fix.
- Partial guard (checking only `candidate_local` or only
  `candidate_domain`): rejected — the upstream commit's own final check
  requires both halves to be present before use; checking only one leaves
  the other free to be NULL when dereferenced downstream.

## Downstream risk

`impact.json.reverse_closure_count` is 1200 (`500-5000` bucket,
`exceeds_threshold = true`); critical downstreams include `curl` and
`openssh`. The change only tightens an existing bounds/NULL check before
any write or dereference, so it cannot alter behavior for any mailbox that
previously parsed successfully.

