  "summary": "Integer underflow (CWE-191) and resultant out-of-bounds read in MIT krb5's NegoEx message parser, reachable when an application calls gss_accept_sec_context() on a system with a NegoEx mechanism registered in /etc/gss/mech. parse_message() in src/lib/gssapi/spnego/negoex_util.c validates a message with `if (in->status || msg_len > token_remaining || header_len > msg_len)`, but this guard never checks header_len against the number of bytes already consumed from the token (in->ptr - msg_base). A crafted NegoEx token can supply a header_len smaller than that consumed amount, so a later computation of the remaining message length (msg_len/header_len arithmetic against the consumed offset) underflows and reads up to 52 bytes past the end of the token buffer. An unauthenticated remote attacker can trigger this pre-authentication, possibly causing the process to terminate; exfiltration of the over-read bytes does not appear possible. Affects MIT krb5 releases 1.18 through 1.22.2 inclusive (fixed in 1.22.3). Upstream fix commit acea6182e46fff3d1d64a3172cdff307b07ca441 (Greg Hudson, 2026-04-08, cherry-picked from 2e75f0d9362fb979f5fc92829431a590a130929f) extends the existing three-conjunct `||` guard with a fourth disjunct, `header_len < (size_t)(in->ptr - msg_base)`, that bounds-checks header_len against the consumed offset before it is used. The same commit also fixes a separate, unrelated NULL-pointer-dereference bug in parse_nego_message() (CVE-2026-40355, a different function and a different hunk); that half is already represented in the corpus as an authoring-only bundle (CVE-2026-40355--krb5-negoex-nullderef) and is out of scope here. The local nixpkgs pin (krb5 1.22.1, pkgs/by-name/kr/krb5/package.nix) carries no NegoEx CVE patch, so it is a valid pre-fix base for this CVE."
}

